.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The skipped command\(cqs exit status is implicitly 0.
This can affect control flow in conditionals.
For example, skipping the test in \f[CR]if test_cmd; then ...\f[R]
causes the \f[CR]then\f[R] branch to execute.
.IP \(bu 2
Skipping a variable assignment means the variable retains its previous
value (or remains unset if it was not previously set).
.IP \(bu 2
Skipping a \f[CR]cd\f[R] command means the working directory does not
change.
.IP \(bu 2
Skip does not work on compound command wrappers (\f[CR]if\f[R],
\f[CR]while\f[R], \f[CR]for\f[R] headers).
It applies to the simple commands within them.
.IP \(bu 2
After skipping, the debugger generates a break\-hit event for the next
command, similar to \f[CR]bc_debug_step()\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Thread safety: the client library is not thread\-safe.
